From: nancc@netcom.com (Nancie Clark)
Date: Sat, 22 Oct 1994 13:08:33 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-10-444] Reproduction

When I introduced the ideas of ectogenesis and the innovations of 
mosaic birth to this conversation, I did so with the idea of  moving 
beyond our  personal views on reproduction, which  is a very 
personal and individual choice.

What excites and stimulates me is discussing the possible future of 
transhumans.  The medical profession has a much greater handle on 
what will be occurring in the field of reproduction than I, but as a 
layperson, as well as a woman, I am tantalized by it.

What would it be like to be able to reproduce biologically when we 
are 70 or 80?  It certainly takes the pressure off.  Pregnancy has 
been an albatross for young women who have been told for eons to 
reproduce while young, plump and pink.  Scary.  How well do we 
know ourselves in the early decades of our lives to be able to even 
know what is healthy to influence others.  At each level, to be sure, 
we think that we are "aware", "enlightened", "clear thinkers"; only 
to find retrospectively that we are somehow shallow in our 
perceptions.  Is there ever a right time to reproduce?  Analytical 
persons will think on this a while, creative folks will say when the 
inspiration hits them.

I would prefer to know what genes I am passing on for the safety of 
the next person who will inherit parts biologically.  If I am prone to 
cancer, I would want this nasty carrier eliminated -- and so on, and 
so forth.  Gene manipulation is a healthy way to manage what goes 
on while reproducing.  Once, I asked a woman who had a child 
with a mental handicap that if while pregnant she was informed that 
the embryo was carrying a "faulty" gene, would she have had it 
altered.  She was horrified.  I would have said, unequivocally, yes!

Mosaic birth is not an art project to be taken lightly, it is a smart, 
intelligent way of engineering a reproduction.  It is actually no 
different that what many women do.  They look around for the 
most handsome and richest man in their townships and latch on -- 
to reproduce what they believe to be a substantial being - with all 
the trimming.  I find the mosaic birth concept clearly having more 
integrity, somehow.

As with all fears, the one concerning whether or not one can allow 
a child her/his independence is viable, yet lessoned by even 
recognizing the matter.  Independence happens.  The more we try 
to curtail it, the stronger the desire for it becomes.  Set an example, 
and let go.

Nancie Clark


